On 2008-01-10, Doug Freyburger <dfreybur@yahoo.com> wrote:
> Derek Ray <de...@moot.its.only.a.spamtrap.org> wrote:
>> Henry J Cobb <hc...@io.com> wrote:
>> > How about switching backstab so it only works on sleeping or paralyzed
>> > critters, instead of scared ones?
>> This has been considered in the past; the problem is that it makes
>> backstab much less of a special bonus for the Rogue class, since
>> obtaining sleeping and/or paralyzed monsters isn't all that easy
>> compared to obtaining fleeing monsters.
> I recall a quote something like <A running man can slit a thousand
> throats in a single night>.  I think back stab should include throat
> slit and thus apply to sleeping, paralysed and scared monsters.

Instakills of helpless monsters isn't a direction I want to go.

Enhancing the +hit bonus and adding a damage bonus, on the other hand,
is certainly under consideration -- but the full one-hit, one-kill is 
not productive since it gives far too much power to the classes who can 
start with the "sleep" spell.

> A player with stealth can already step into a barracks or whatever
> and grind through almost all of the monsters in the room without
> waking the rest.  

I agree that this is a problem, and changes have already been made in Spork;
specifically, "fighting makes noise" (with special bonuses for stealthy
players, Rogues, and anyone using a knife).  Stealthy players and Rogues
are less likely to wake things up; knives are silent.  But hacking
through a roomful of sleeping monsters one by one is no longer so easy
as all that.

(There is a loophole right now in that pet attacks don't yet make noise;
I don't think this is particularly abusable in any consistent manner
since even large dogs have monsters they won't attack, but just for
internal consistency some sort of noise needs to be made, I think.)

> Giving a rogue an extra advantage like this would not matter enough
> to bother with the change if the only time you encounter sleeping
> monsters was a barracks/zoo.  

A number of monsters are generated sleeping; if you're a doorkicker you
don't notice this sort of thing, of course.  My goal is, at least in
part, to make intentionally avoiding noisy actions more worthwhile;
this could mean that OOD monsters might start to be generated but also 
start out sleeping.

>> > That would nerf out about half of Magicbane's appeal to Rogues.
>> This is begging the question; why should Magicbane _not_ appeal to Rogues?
> Since it can engrave the E-word without degrading and it is in the
> dagger class, Magicbane can appeal to ANY character.  I've even
> spent a wish on an athame for an otherwise well equiped combat
> wombat because of that.

That's my point.  Magicbane isn't bad for anyone, given its special gift
regarding curses, ability to generate infinite Elbereth, and disruption
effects to creatures.

For Rogues, Magicbane requires a wish, self-conversion, or a bones file;
none of these are particularly trivial.  I see little reason to make it
suddenly less effective unless there's something actually
game-unbalancing about a Rogue with Magicbane.

In vanilla, there were some issues, but these no longer exist in Spork;
resistable Elbereth and no-thrown-backstab take care of the two most
obvious problems, and those weren't Magicbane-specific in the first
place; an ordinary athame would have done as well.

> The main reason I can think of for disinterest in Magicbane is role
> playing - For any character who gets a special weapon as the first
> gift, use that for the rest of the game because it's appropriate.

That's fine for individual players who choose to remain "appropriate",
but Henry seems to think that changes should be made to intentionally
reduce Magicbane's appeal to Rogues; I'm trying to discover why.

> Search the archives carefully and I think you'll find a racial orc who
> made a point of using Grimtooth from the time he got it as a gift.

Didn't Harold whose-last-name-I-cannot-remember do this for his orc,
since Grimtooth is probably the best "base-item-orcish" weapon?

